From 676437a978338b28d8eadb12ac0ee4e23e0e9d26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Benjamin Larsson <banan@ludd.ltu.se>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 12:22:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: give untagged xPON frames the default priority

An OLT commonly expresses its entire upstream service graph through an
802.1p mapper, one service per p-bit. The transmit classifier reports a
priority only for a frame that carries a VLAN tag, so an untagged frame is
looked up with no priority at all and matches none of those services. It is
then dropped in the transmit path, before a descriptor is built and before
the hardware sees it.

An untagged frame does have a priority: the default of zero, for which the
mapper has an entry. Look it up that way, so untagged upstream traffic takes
the GEM port and T-CONT the OLT provisioned for priority zero.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt Project <openwrt-devel@lists.openwrt.org>
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth_gen1.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth_gen1.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth_gen1.c
index 6049b693b310..1012b0d29916 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth_gen1.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth_gen1.c
@@ -427,8 +427,16 @@ static int econet_xpon_classify(struct econet_gdm_port *port,
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * An untagged frame still carries a priority: it is the default of
+	 * zero, and the 802.1p mapper an OLT provisions has an entry for it.
+	 * Treating such a frame as having no priority at all leaves it
+	 * matching no service on an OLT that expresses its whole service graph
+	 * through that mapper, and it is then dropped before ever reaching the
+	 * hardware.
+	 */
 	return econet_xpon_lookup_service(port, vlan_valid, vlan_id,
-					  vlan_valid, pcp, info);
+					  true, pcp, info);
 }
 
 static netdev_tx_t econet_dev_xmit(struct sk_buff *skb, struct net_device *dev)
-- 
